<pdd-reason>Architecture and pdd-interface conformance: verifies that generated code actually exports the symbols its architecture entry and its prompt declare, with the declared parameter shapes.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-interface>
{
  "type": "module",
  "module": {
    "functions": [
      {"name": "_verify_architecture_conformance", "signature": "(generated_code: str, prompt_name: str, language: str, output_path: Optional[str] = None, prompt_content: Optional[str] = None) -> None", "returns": "None"},
      {"name": "_verify_pdd_interface_signatures", "signature": "(generated_code: str, prompt_name: str, prompt_content: Optional[str], output_path: Optional[str] = None) -> None", "returns": "None"},
      {"name": "_collect_pdd_interface_names", "signature": "(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> Set[str]", "returns": "Set[str]"}
    ]
  }
}
</pdd-interface>
<pdd-dependency>conformance/gate_errors_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the architecture and
% `<pdd-interface>` conformance checks in `pdd/conformance/interface_check.py`.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Responsibility
  Confirming that generated code exports what it promised - to `architecture.json`
  and to its own prompt - and that declared callables have the declared parameters.

% Non-responsibilities
  Does not compare against the PREVIOUS generation; that is the public-surface
  gate. Does not judge naming beyond the camelCase backstop below.

% Vocabulary
  - **declared symbol**: a name in `architecture.json`'s interface or in the
    prompt's `<pdd-interface>`.
  - **drift**: a declared parameter that exists but whose annotation or default
    no longer matches the declaration.

% Contract rules
<contract_rules>
  R1. Symbol collection MUST NOT descend into `if`, `try` or `with` blocks inside
      a class body. Conformance is a hard validator and MUST NOT accept a method
      whose existence depends on branch evaluation.
  R2. Class members MUST be reported as dotted names at every depth, so a method
      on `Outer.Inner` is `Outer.Inner.method`.
  R3. Three `<pdd-interface>` shapes carry callable signatures and MUST all be
      inspected: `module` functions, `cli` commands, and `command` entries in
      either the multi-command or single-command form. An entry with no signature
      is a silent no-op.
  R4. A dotted declaration MUST be resolved by descending nested classes by name
      and matching the final segment as a method.
  R5. Three failure categories MUST be checked in priority order:
      missing function or method; missing parameter; then signature drift.
      Reporting a missing symbol from the architecture entry first means the same
      symbol is never reported twice.
  R6. Every declared parameter name MUST be present. Variadic `*args` and
      `**kwargs` MUST NOT satisfy a declared named parameter - the prompt is the
      source of truth and callers pass the parameter by name. Missing parameters
      are reported as `function.parameter`.
  R7. Annotation drift is CONSERVATIVE: it MUST raise only when both sides
      annotate and the two are not semantically compatible. Interchangeable
      spellings - typing aliases, equivalent optionals - are not drift, and an
      annotation missing on one side only never fires, since gradually-typed code
      may catch up later.
  R8. Default drift is STRICT: raise when the declaration gives a default and the
      generated code drops it or gives one that is not provably the same value.
      An unresolvable default MUST fail closed. Defaults are runtime behavior -
      a caller omitting the declared optional argument breaks with `TypeError`
      when the default disappears or changes.
  R9. Each category MUST occupy its own sentence in the message, so the
      subprocess parser can route them: declared functions or methods missing;
      declared parameters missing; declared parameters whose signature drifted,
      each naming the symbol, the kind of drift, and the declared and found text.
      All three sets combine into the reported missing symbols so retry callers
      see the full failure.
  R10. The repair directive MUST attribute a dotted parameter to its FULL
       function name: `ContentSelector.select.mode` is parameter `mode` of
       `ContentSelector.select`, never parameter `select.mode` of class
       `ContentSelector`. Splitting on the first dot instead of the last produces
       exactly that error. The directive MUST NOT suggest deleting the declared
       parameter from the prompt.
  R11. The camelCase backstop MUST exempt any export whose exact name is declared
       in EITHER the architecture entry OR the prompt's own `<pdd-interface>`,
       flagging only undeclared camelCase exports. Declared camelCase names are
       intentional public API, such as Firebase Cloud Function exports, not
       accidental drift (#1446). The exemption is scoped to the naming check and
       MUST NOT weaken the missing-symbol check.
  R12. `_collect_pdd_interface_names` MUST return every declared name including
       description-only entries, since the exemption keys on the name alone. It
       MUST NOT reuse the signature-gated collector, and MUST return nothing when
       the prompt or its interface is absent or malformed - it never blocks
       generation.
  R13. A missing `<pdd-interface>` MUST skip the signature check silently. A
       malformed one MUST log a warning and skip; it MUST NOT raise.
  R14. All failures MUST raise `ArchitectureConformanceError` carrying the
       prompt name, output path, matched architecture entry, and the expected,
       found and missing symbol sets, so retry callers can build a repair
       directive without parsing the message.
</contract_rules>

% Inputs and outputs
  Symbol extraction is AST-based for Python and pattern-based for
  TypeScript/JavaScript exports.

% Dependencies
  Standard library `ast`, `json`, `logging`, `re`;
  `ArchitectureConformanceError` from `.gate_errors`; and the annotation and default
  comparators plus module default-symbol tables from `pdd.interface_semantics`.
